.TH AT 1
.SH NAME
at \- queue, list or cancel one-shot shell jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B at
[\-s spool] [\-f file] time [date]
.br
.B at
[\-s spool] [\-f file] \-t YYYYMMDDhhmm[.ss]
.br
.B at
[\-s spool] {\-l | \-r job-id}
.SH DESCRIPTION
Queue commands from standard input or -f file. The script captures the
working directory, shell-compatible exported environment variables, and umask.
Captured values and paths are shell-quoted. IFS, ENV and BASH_ENV are excluded.
The original standard input after submission is not retained for execution.
The command prints job followed by the job ID after atomic publication.
.SH TIMES
.nf
at now
at now + 5 minutes
at now + 2 hours
at now + 1 day
at 14:30
at 14:30 2028-02-29
at -t 202802291430.00
.fi
HH:MM alone selects its next future occurrence. Explicit dates cannot be
in the past. A relative day means 86400 seconds. Calendar dates use the local
TZ or /etc/TZ rules; nonexistent DST local times are rejected. Timestamps are
limited to the current unsigned 32-bit guest epoch range, through 2106.
.SH QUEUE
The default spool is /var/spool/at with private jobs, running and output
subdirectories. -s selects an absolute alternate spool, at most 223 native
characters long so complete job paths fit the current 256-character path budget. The spool and its
subdirectories must be owned by the effective user and have no group/other
permissions. There are no setuid binaries or cross-user submissions: use the
system queue as root or a private queue and runner under your own account.
On the default installed system cron invokes atrun once per minute when enabled.
.PP
-l lists pending jobs and local due times. -r removes one pending job by ID;
claimed jobs cannot be cancelled with this interface.
.SH RESULTS
Each executed job's stdout and stderr go to spool/output/job-id, retained
for inspection even after failure. Completed jobs are removed from running.
The queue is at-most-once: interruption after claiming may leave a job in
running; inspect it and its output before explicitly resubmitting. Automatic
replay could duplicate external side effects and is deliberately avoided.
.PP
Submission writes a private temporary script, flushes it and publishes it
through a hard link. Crashes may leave unpublished .t temporary files. Runtime
claiming prevents duplicate execution by competing runners; full transactional
power-loss recovery depends on the filesystem and is not guaranteed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 for queue, file or output failures; 2 for invalid options/times.
.SH SEE ALSO
atrun(1), cron(1)
